# Semantic Kernel Agents 101

This notebook demonstrates how to create and interact with AI agents using Semantic Kernel. We'll create an agent that can assist with tasks and use tools/plugins.

## What are Agents?

Agents in Semantic Kernel are AI-powered assistants that can:
- Maintain conversation context
- Use tools and plugins to perform actions
- Follow specific instructions and personalities
- Collaborate with other agents

#!csharp

// Import Semantic Kernel packages
#r "nuget: Microsoft.SemanticKernel, 1.29.0"
#r "nuget: Microsoft.SemanticKernel.Agents.Core, 1.29.0-alpha"
#r "nuget: Microsoft.SemanticKernel.Plugins.Core, 1.29.0-alpha"

#!markdown

## Setup and Configuration

First, we need to configure our AI service connection. This example uses Azure OpenAI, but you can also use OpenAI.

#!csharp

using Microsoft.SemanticKernel;
using Microsoft.SemanticKernel.Agents;
using Microsoft.SemanticKernel.ChatCompletion;
using Microsoft.SemanticKernel.Connectors.OpenAI;
using System;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

// Configuration - Replace with your actual values
var deploymentName = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable("AZURE_OPENAI_DEPLOYMENT_NAME") ?? "gpt-4";
var endpoint = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable("AZURE_OPENAI_ENDPOINT") ?? "your-endpoint";
var apiKey = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable("AZURE_OPENAI_API_KEY") ?? "your-api-key";

Console.WriteLine("Configuration loaded successfully!");

#!markdown

## Create the Kernel

The Kernel is the core of Semantic Kernel that manages AI services and plugins.

#!csharp

// Create a kernel with Azure OpenAI chat completion
var builder = Kernel.CreateBuilder();

builder.AddAzureOpenAIChatCompletion(
    deploymentName: deploymentName,
    endpoint: endpoint,
    apiKey: apiKey
);

var kernel = builder.Build();

Console.WriteLine("Kernel created successfully!");

#!markdown

## Define Agent Instructions

Let's create an agent with specific instructions and personality.

#!csharp

const string AgentName = "Assistant";
const string AgentInstructions = @"
You are a helpful AI assistant. Your role is to:
- Answer questions accurately and concisely
- Help users accomplish their tasks
- Be friendly and professional
- Ask clarifying questions when needed
";

Console.WriteLine($"Agent '{AgentName}' instructions defined.");

#!markdown

## Create the Chat Completion Agent

Now we'll create the actual agent using the ChatCompletionAgent class.

#!csharp

// Create the agent
ChatCompletionAgent agent = new()
{
    Name = AgentName,
    Instructions = AgentInstructions,
    Kernel = kernel
};

Console.WriteLine($"Agent '{agent.Name}' created successfully!");

#!markdown

## Create a Chat History

We'll maintain a chat history to keep track of the conversation.

#!csharp

using Microsoft.SemanticKernel.ChatCompletion;

var chatHistory = new ChatHistory();

Console.WriteLine("Chat history initialized.");

#!markdown

## Interact with the Agent

Let's have a conversation with our agent!

#!csharp

// Helper function to chat with the agent
async Task ChatWithAgent(string userMessage)
{
    Console.WriteLine($"\n👤 User: {userMessage}");
    chatHistory.AddUserMessage(userMessage);
    
    // Get agent response
    await foreach (var content in agent.InvokeAsync(chatHistory))
    {
        chatHistory.Add(content);
        Console.WriteLine($"🤖 {agent.Name}: {content.Content}");
    }
}

// Example conversation
await ChatWithAgent("Hello! Who are you?");

#!csharp

// Continue the conversation
await ChatWithAgent("What can you help me with?");

#!csharp

// Ask a specific question
await ChatWithAgent("Can you explain what Semantic Kernel is in simple terms?");

#!markdown

## Adding Plugins to the Agent

Agents become more powerful when they can use tools. Let's add a plugin with some useful functions.

#!csharp

using Microsoft.SemanticKernel;
using System.ComponentModel;

// Define a simple plugin with tools
public class MathPlugin
{
    [KernelFunction, Description("Adds two numbers together")]
    public int Add(
        [Description("The first number")] int a,
        [Description("The second number")] int b)
    {
        return a + b;
    }
    
    [KernelFunction, Description("Multiplies two numbers together")]
    public int Multiply(
        [Description("The first number")] int a,
        [Description("The second number")] int b)
    {
        return a * b;
    }
}

// Add the plugin to the kernel
kernel.Plugins.AddFromType<MathPlugin>();

Console.WriteLine("MathPlugin added to the kernel!");

#!csharp

// Create a new agent with tool calling enabled
ChatCompletionAgent agentWithTools = new()
{
    Name = "MathAssistant",
    Instructions = @"
You are a helpful math assistant. You have access to tools for basic math operations.
When asked to perform calculations, use the available tools.
Always explain what you're doing.",
    Kernel = kernel,
    Arguments = new KernelArguments(new OpenAIPromptExecutionSettings 
    { 
        ToolCallBehavior = ToolCallBehavior.AutoInvokeKernelFunctions 
    })
};

Console.WriteLine($"Agent '{agentWithTools.Name}' created with tools!");

#!csharp

// Start a new conversation with the tool-enabled agent
var toolChatHistory = new ChatHistory();

async Task ChatWithToolAgent(string userMessage)
{
    Console.WriteLine($"\n👤 User: {userMessage}");
    toolChatHistory.AddUserMessage(userMessage);
    
    await foreach (var content in agentWithTools.InvokeAsync(toolChatHistory))
    {
        toolChatHistory.Add(content);
        Console.WriteLine($"🤖 {agentWithTools.Name}: {content.Content}");
    }
}

await ChatWithToolAgent("What is 45 plus 67?");

#!csharp

await ChatWithToolAgent("Now multiply the result by 3");

#!markdown

## View Chat History

Let's examine the full conversation history to see how the agent maintains context.

#!csharp

Console.WriteLine("\n📜 Full Chat History:");
Console.WriteLine("=".PadRight(50, '='));

foreach (var message in toolChatHistory)
{
    var role = message.Role.ToString().ToUpper();
    var content = message.Content ?? "[No content]";
    Console.WriteLine($"\n[{role}]");
    Console.WriteLine(content);
}

